// The `standard:` tier — classical results the manuscript COLLECTS rather than proves.
//
// The author's own appendices say so in their titles: "Preparations from Algebraic Number
// Theory", "Preparations from Analytic Number Theory", "Preparations on locally convex
// topological vector spaces". ~400 pages of textbook material gathered for the reader's
// convenience. Each result here is cited with a real source, not merely waved at: a reader
// has to be able to check it.
//
// A `cites` entry is a POSITION, not a permanent verdict. Each of these can be formalized
// later on its own schedule, and when one lands nothing downstream changes — the interface
// its consumers depend on is unaffected. That is what makes citing-first reversible rather
// than a shortcut. Everything here stays visible in `--axioms`.
//
// ALTITUDE: opaque, like the rest of this formalization. These are the INTERFACES the
// manuscript uses, not the theorems themselves.

theory vocab <<< "../shared/vocabulary.b4m"

sort TestFunction = vocab.TestFunction
/// a measurable set / group element locus — opaque
sort Locus
/// a function on the idele class group, in the manuscript's Hardy-Sobolev scale
sort HardySobolevFunction

/// the Lebesgue-differentiation property holds at this locus: averages over shrinking
/// neighbourhoods converge to the value almost everywhere
pred differentiatesAlmostEverywhere(l: Locus)
/// the Hardy-Littlewood maximal operator is bounded on this function
pred hasBoundedMaximalFunction(h: HardySobolevFunction)
/// the Poisson summation formula applies, in the jump-singularity form the manuscript needs
pred poissonSummationApplies(f: TestFunction)

intheory lebesgueDifferentiation

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CLASSICAL — cited, with sources. The manuscript restates these in Appendix A.5 / B.4.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Lebesgue's Differentiation Theorem over a Hausdorff locally compact abelian group.
/// Manuscript Theorem A.5.4.1 (Appendix A.5, "Preparations from Algebraic Number Theory").
hole lebesgueDifferentiationTheorem
  cites "standard: Lebesgue differentiation over a Hausdorff locally compact abelian group — Federer, Geometric Measure Theory §2.9; Hewitt-Ross, Abstract Harmonic Analysis I §44. Manuscript Thm A.5.4.1":
  forall l: Locus; differentiatesAlmostEverywhere(l)

/// The Hardy-Littlewood maximal theorem, global form.
/// Manuscript Theorem A.5.3.7.
hole hardyLittlewoodMaximalTheorem
  cites "standard: Hardy-Littlewood maximal inequality — Stein-Weiss, Introduction to Fourier Analysis on Euclidean Spaces Ch. II; the locally compact group form in Hewitt-Ross I. Manuscript Thm A.5.3.7":
  forall h: HardySobolevFunction; hasBoundedMaximalFunction(h)

/// The Poisson summation formula in the jump-singularity form.
/// Manuscript Proposition B.4.1.2.
hole poissonSummationFormula
  cites "standard: Poisson summation, with the jump-singularity refinement — Tate's thesis (Cassels-Frohlich Ch. XV); Weil, Basic Number Theory §VII. Manuscript Prop B.4.1.2":
  forall f: TestFunction; isTestFunction(f) -> poissonSummationApplies(f)

// A trivially-true consequence, so this file has a THEOREM and its holes are not dead
// weight — an uncited hole nothing depends on is invisible to `--axioms`, which would
// defeat the purpose of recording them.
theorem lebesgueDifferentiation: forall l: Locus; differentiatesAlmostEverywhere(l)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall l: Locus; differentiatesAlmostEverywhere(l)
    [by cite lebesgueDifferentiationTheorem]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ANALYTIC NUMBER THEORY — Appendix B's "Preparations". Classical.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// L(s, chi) has no zero on the line Re(s) = 1
pred noZeroOnTheOneLine()

intheory nonVanishingOnTheOneLine

/// Non-vanishing of the Hecke L-function on the 1-line. The author calls this an INPUT in
/// his own words (txt 819: "the non-vanishing of L(s, chi) on the 1-line is used as an
/// input"), and §1.7.2 lists "properties of the Hecke L-function on the 1-line (including
/// the non-vanishing)" among the things "used as an input throughout".
///
/// Classical: Hadamard-de la Vallee Poussin for zeta, extended to Hecke L-functions. The
/// manuscript's own quantitative versions are Thms B.7.2.1 / B.7.2.4 (Euler products on the
/// 1-line), which are estimates rather than quotable statements — so what is cited here is
/// the classical fact they refine, not those theorems.
hole nonVanishingOnTheOneLineHolds
  cites "standard: L(s, chi) != 0 on Re(s) = 1 — Hadamard and de la Vallee Poussin for zeta; the Hecke L-function case in Iwaniec-Kowalski, Analytic Number Theory Thm 5.10, or Lang, Algebraic Number Theory XV §4. The author calls it an INPUT at txt 819 and §1.7.2":
  noZeroOnTheOneLine()

theorem nonVanishingOnTheOneLine: noZeroOnTheOneLine()
proof
  @conclusion |
    noZeroOnTheOneLine()
    [by cite nonVanishingOnTheOneLineHolds]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE THEORY — additivity, which the kernel decomposition is an instance of.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// an increasing family of sets exhausting its ambient space
pred isANestedExhaustion(l: Locus)
/// the successive differences of a nested exhaustion are pairwise disjoint and cover
pred successiveDifferencesPartition(l: Locus)

/// the domain IS partitioned exactly when the successive differences do: DEFINED, not a
/// second opaque notion, so no glue hole is needed to get from one to the other. (An earlier
/// draft had both as opaque predicates plus a hole relating them — which checked nothing.)
pred partitionsTheDomain(l: Locus):
  partitionsTheDomain(l) iff successiveDifferencesPartition(l).
/// integrating over a partition and summing agrees with integrating over the whole
pred integralIsAdditiveOver(l: Locus)

intheory countableAdditivity

/// Countable additivity of the Haar integral over a measurable partition. This is what
/// equation (11.3.1.4) USES: that equation is a DEFINITION — it defines k_{p_k} as the
/// successive difference (alpha_{k+1} - alpha_k) * k_geom — and the Trace Formula's proof
/// then integrates term by term and sums. The mathematical content of that move is
/// additivity, not anything specific to this manuscript.
hole theIntegralIsCountablyAdditive
  cites "standard: countable additivity of a Haar integral over a measurable partition — Halmos, Measure Theory §25; Hewitt-Ross, Abstract Harmonic Analysis I §15. Used by the manuscript at eq (11.3.1.4) + txt 31550, where the alpha_k indicators chop the ideles along the divisorial filtration":
  forall l: Locus; partitionsTheDomain(l) -> integralIsAdditiveOver(l)

theorem countableAdditivity: forall l: Locus;
  partitionsTheDomain(l) -> integralIsAdditiveOver(l)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall l: Locus; partitionsTheDomain(l) -> integralIsAdditiveOver(l)
    [by cite theIntegralIsCountablyAdditive]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MOLLIFIERS AND BUMP FUNCTIONS — the §1.4.2 construction's classical ingredients.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// a smooth compactly supported function on R_{>0}
sort SmoothCompactlySupported

/// convolving with a mollifier smooths a function and converges to it pointwise as eps -> 0
pred mollifierConvolutionSmoothsAndConverges(g: SmoothCompactlySupported)
/// a bump function supported in (1, +infinity) and valued in [0,1] exists
pred bumpFunctionsExist()

intheory mollifiersSmoothAndBumpsExist

/// Existence of smooth bump functions with prescribed support, and that convolution with a
/// mollifier smooths while converging pointwise. The manuscript's §1.4.2 builds
/// `phi_{h,eps,z} := h(t) * (rho_eps * phi_z)` out of exactly these two, so they are the
/// construction's classical ingredients — not the part that is specific to this proof.
hole mollifiersAndBumpFunctionsExist
  cites "standard: smooth bump functions with prescribed support, and mollifier convolution converging pointwise — Hormander, The Analysis of Linear Partial Differential Operators I §1.2-1.4; Rudin, Functional Analysis Thm 6.20. The manuscript builds phi_{h,eps,z} from these at §1.4.2":
  bumpFunctionsExist() and
  (forall g: SmoothCompactlySupported; mollifierConvolutionSmoothsAndConverges(g))

theorem mollifiersSmoothAndBumpsExist: bumpFunctionsExist()
proof
  @both |
    bumpFunctionsExist() and
      (forall g: SmoothCompactlySupported; mollifierConvolutionSmoothsAndConverges(g))
    [by cite mollifiersAndBumpFunctionsExist]
  @conclusion |
    bumpFunctionsExist()
    [by and_lhs both]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPLEX ANALYSIS — the logarithmic-derivative principle.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// a point of the complex plane where L might vanish
sort Point

/// L vanishes at this point
pred lVanishesAt(z: Point)
/// L'/L has a pole at this point
pred logDerivativeHasAPoleAt(z: Point)

intheory aZeroForcesAPoleOfTheLogDerivative

/// A zero of a meromorphic function forces a POLE of its logarithmic derivative, with
/// residue the multiplicity. Classical, and it is the principle the manuscript's §1.4.5 uses
/// without naming: having shown L'_f/L_f holomorphic on C_{>0} minus {1/2}, it concludes
/// "this implies that L_f(s, chi) ... has no zeros over C_{>1/2}" — i.e. contrapositive of
/// this fact.
hole aZeroForcesAPole
  cites "standard: if f is meromorphic and f(rho) = 0 with multiplicity m, then f'/f has a simple pole at rho with residue m — Ahlfors, Complex Analysis Ch. 4 §3; Conway, Functions of One Complex Variable VIII §1. The manuscript uses the contrapositive at §1.4.5 without naming it":
  forall z: Point; lVanishesAt(z) -> logDerivativeHasAPoleAt(z)

theorem aZeroForcesAPoleOfTheLogDerivative:
  forall z: Point; lVanishesAt(z) -> logDerivativeHasAPoleAt(z)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall z: Point; lVanishesAt(z) -> logDerivativeHasAPoleAt(z)
    [by cite aZeroForcesAPole]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PREIMAGES AND NESTED EXHAUSTIONS — why a stratification pulls back to a partition.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

intheory nestedExhaustionsPartition

/// The successive differences of a NESTED EXHAUSTION partition: if `A_0 subset A_1 subset …`
/// with union the whole space, then `{A_{k+1} - A_k}` are pairwise disjoint and cover.
/// Elementary set theory, and it is exactly why the alpha_k = 1_{A_{k+1}} - 1_{A_k}
/// indicators chop the ideles cleanly.
///
/// The manuscript's strata are the PREIMAGES under the norm map GL_1(A_F) -> GL_1(A_Q) of
/// the divisorial strata on GL_1(A_Q) (txt 1166-1169), with the P_k nested and exhausting by
/// construction — and a preimage of a partition is a partition, which is the second half of
/// why this applies.
hole nestedExhaustionsHaveDisjointDifferences
  cites "standard: the successive differences of an increasing exhaustion are pairwise disjoint and cover; and the preimage of a partition under any map is a partition — elementary set theory (e.g. Halmos, Naive Set Theory §9; Halmos, Measure Theory §5 for the measurable version). The manuscript's strata are norm-map preimages of the GL_1(A_Q) divisorial strata, txt 1166-1169":
  forall l: Locus; isANestedExhaustion(l) -> successiveDifferencesPartition(l)

theorem nestedExhaustionsPartition:
  forall l: Locus; isANestedExhaustion(l) -> successiveDifferencesPartition(l)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall l: Locus; isANestedExhaustion(l) -> successiveDifferencesPartition(l)
    [by cite nestedExhaustionsHaveDisjointDifferences]
qed
